WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:13.270 align:start position:0%
 
the animal  control by law is being changed because  we're trying to put in  a bit of harsher restrictions on people  that we have  consistent problems with so the old  bylaw  is quite vague we're changing things  like  people need unimpeded access to their  front door or a  door to their dwelling and this is for  safety issues that we've run across  such as service workers  council employees have been attacked by  dogs postal workers things of that  nature  what we're finding right now is a lot of  our dog enforcement officers are  spending most of their time dealing with  problems  and so they're enforcing constantly and  they don't get the chance to educate the  public  so our hope is with a stronger bylaw  that we can enforce their infringements  and other actions  we can get the public and the people  that are a problem to be taken care of  as quickly as possible  so i think what's really important for  us to get across to people is these  stronger bylaws are not punishing the  people who are good dog owners  the majority of dog owners in  invercargill are extremely responsible  people  and they're very caring for their pets  and they're also very conscious of their  neighbors and citizens within  invercargill  the bylaws are really getting  strengthened to deal with the problem  issues that we have or maybe repeat  offenders  if we have stronger bylaws it allows us  to enforce  without having to spend lots of time on  it it just cuts the problem out and it  gives the dog officers  more time to spend doing the more  positive things of their roles  people are really surprised to find out  that we average  one dog for every five people in  invercargill that's a statistic that's  actually extremely high  and that's what we know as far as  registered dogs we estimate that there's  another 900 to 1000 dogs that aren't  registered which would bring that  average up  so because of that we have to have  strong enough bylaws in place so when  there is a problem we can deal with it  as quickly as possible  so some of our key changes that we are  doing in the new bylaw  are we would like that all dogs  registered as menacing  are neutered within 30 days of receiving  that notification  that means that after they've gone if  the owner decides to appeal  and if they failed the appeal within 30  days that dog would have to be neutered  currently that is not in the bylaw we're  also asking that all  dogs that are registered as dangerous  must be neutered within 30 days of that  classification as well  um other things that we're changing is  we're defining  under control as being on a lead  so whereas before we actually never  defined what under control means we are  looking at possibly adding  through the submission process under  control being a voice command as well  because some dogs do respond  differently to voice command than they  do on lead  we're also including maps that will give  a defined area of where you can have  your dog  off a leash because previously it's been  a bit confusing so we were thinking by  providing full color maps people have a  really  good idea of where they're allowed to  have their dog off lead  a lot of people don't understand where  they can actually have the dog on and  off lead  like queen's park is a great example you  actually need to have your dog on lead  but it's also the things we want to  clarify in the bylaw and  within the policy are for people who are  non-dog owners as well  so another major change that we've made  is we're saying that  you cannot have a dog that intimidates  people at your gate  for example if you're a citizen walking  down the street whether you're a dog  owner or a non-dog owner  if you are constantly being having a dog  aggressively  go at you at that fence and it becomes a  problem and it's a repeated problem  amongst  more than one person it's not an obvious  complaint by somebody who just doesn't  like your dog  then we've put that in that we will  actually take actions to ask you to  either move your gate back  or to do things put things in place that  will help  make it less of a problem because one of  the things we found  is that we don't really have any powers  to change the problems and what we want  to do through our new model is called  evade so our first step would be to  educate people  so in that situation our first step  would be to go talk to the owner  say look there's been a problem there's  multiple reports we would really like to  work with you to fix it  before we would start writing  infringement notices or sending out  warnings  so under control in a vehicle to us is  means that the dog is actually tied up  properly  or put in the car with enough air but  cannot be released from the vehicle  or it needs to be kept in a kennel in  the back of the vehicle or in a truck it  actually needs to be restrained within a  kennel  it can't be just sitting in the back  free and that's also for the dog safety  not just people safety because if you  stop suddenly  or you were to have an accident in your  car and your dog isn't properly kindled  in the back of your truck  obviously the dog could be victim to  that accident in our current bylaw  you can have three dogs and then you  don't need a license to get a  then you'll need a license to get a  fourth dog in the new bylaw you will  have two dogs  and you will need a license to get a  third some people are a little bit  confused about that  so we have a grandfathering enroll so if  you currently  have three dogs that will not affect you  when you register your dogs you'll be  under the same registration system as  you were before  however if you have two dogs and you're  getting a third dog after july  you will have to apply for the special  license fee  also if you say have three dogs now  and you're registering all three in july  but maybe one dies in a year or two  or or something happens and you get a  third dog again  then you will have to apply under the  new system so the submission process has  been open  from the 5th of april and it goes until  the 8th of may  for the submissions we've gone out to  the invercargill city dog park  which is over in south city and we set  up for a day with one of our dog control  officers and we were answering questions  to anyone that had  issues or wanted to find out more  information  so all you need to do is get a  submission form you can get it printed  out on the invicargo city council  website  or you can go into one of the invicargo  city buildings so you can get it from  the library  the esk street building or in bluff at  the community center  once you fill out your submission you  just return it  you can either send it in by post or you  can bring it in by hand  you can also email your submission in  once we receive them we will go through  them  we will be making modifications based on  the submissions if we get a lot of  issues  that we find are really relevant to a  great number of people then we look at  changing those before we send the final  one through  it will go to council you'll have the  opportunity to present to them  and once you do that then we make those  changes based on what council is heard  so what's going to happen is once the  changes go through everything will be  effective for the next financial year  so basically with your index dog  registration those changes will be  implemented  you

